【July 28 Global Market News and Data Analysis】
1、#FOMC‬⁩  rate decision preview: Standing pat remains the baseline; the risk of an unexpected rate hike cannot be ignored;
2、Ukrainian President Zelensky arrives in the U.S. and will hold talks with #TRUMP ;
3、Analysis: #BTC  and #ETH  are expected to bottom out in different time windows during this round;
4、Investment banks: High-net-worth investors still retain allocations to crypto assets, but funds are shifting toward gold, deposits, and alternative assets.

This week’s Federal Reserve rate decision coincides with earnings reports from tech giants, creating the biggest uncertainty for the U.S. stock market. The federal funds rate is maintained at 3.50%—3.75%. Whether it stays put or delivers an unexpected hike is a matter of debate among mainstream institutions. Goldman Sachs believes softer inflation data reduces the necessity for further hikes, and the Fed has historically rarely taken sudden action. JPMorgan Chase, meanwhile, expects a continued pause throughout the year, with any rate hike potentially delayed until 2027. However, some caution that factors such as steady employment, high oil prices, and tariff pressures may prompt the Fed to act earlier. In the market, pricing for the probability of a rate hike has risen to roughly 30%—40%.
From the crypto market perspective, expectations of liquidity tightening driven by rate uncertainty are continuing to weigh on Bitcoin. Historical data show that aggressive hiking cycles previously caused Bitcoin to retrace by more than 60% from its highs. If there is an unexpected rate hike this week or hawkish signals are released, risk assets will be pressured first, and Bitcoin could accelerate in breaking below technical support. Even if rates are kept unchanged, if the statement’s wording leans restrictive, it would likewise suppress market risk appetite and could slow inflows into crypto. Of note is that some views suggest ETH has shown greater resilience relative to BTC; if BTC continues operating below an ascending channel, its downside pressure may be greater than that of Ethereum. Overall, until the Fed’s policy path becomes clearer, the high-volatility regime for crypto assets is unlikely to change.

